  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Interior obligated $6.8B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department recorded 25,180 awards with an average award size of $270K, and obligations were up 3.5% year over year. The Department's FY21 to FY26 obligations show a multi-year pattern: FY21 was $5.1B, FY22 was $6.5B, FY23 was $7.6B, FY24 was $8.1B, FY25 was $6.6B, and FY26 is $6.8B. Construction and IT services dominate the FY2026 to date spend by NAICS: Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction accounted for $752M, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $542M, and Other Computer Related Services accounted for $520M. The top vendors for FY2026 to date include GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $239M, GUIDEHOUSE INC. at $136M, and AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC at $132M. Contract obligations are concentrated in a mix of heavy civil construction, building trades, and information technology and consulting services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What are the biggest spending categories for Interior this year?",
        "body": "Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction is the largest category at $752M. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction follows at $542M, with Other Computer Related Services at $520M and Computer Systems Design Services at $516M. Other significant categories include Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $494M and Support Activities for Forestry at $288M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who captures the largest share of Interior contract dollars?",
        "body": "GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is the top vendor at $239M. GUIDEHOUSE INC. is second at $136M and AMES FEDERAL CONTRACTING GROUP LLC is third at $132M. RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. and NW CONSTRUCTION, INC rank next at $130M and $127M respectively."
      }
    ]
